  "SCALED INGREDIENTS:\n*   **Eggplant:** 25 lbs (approx. 20-22 large), 1/2-inch dice\n*   **Zucchini:** 15 lbs, 1/2-inch dice\n*   **Yellow Squash:** 15 lbs, 1/2-inch dice\n*   **Red & Yellow Bell Peppers:** 12 lbs (approx. 24 peppers), 1/2-inch dice\n*   **Yellow Onions:** 10 lbs, small dice\n*   **Garlic:** 1 lb, minced\n*   **Canned Crushed Tomatoes (Peeled):** 3x #10 cans (approx. 2.25 gallons)\n*   **Extra Virgin Olive Oil:** 1.5 liters\n*   **Fresh Thyme:** 4 bunches, tied (or 4 oz leaves)\n*   **Fresh Basil:** 2 lbs, chiffonade (for finishing)\n*   **Kosher Salt:** 7 oz (approx. 1 cup), adjust to taste (calculated at 75% linear scale)\n*   **Black Pepper:** 2 oz\n*   **Red Wine Vinegar:** 1 cup (to brighten at finish)\n\nREVISED TECHNIQUE:\nTo avoid the \"mush\" effect common in large-batch ratatouille, do not stew everything together from raw. \n1. **The Roasting Method:** Toss diced eggplant, zucchini, squash, and peppers in olive oil and salt. Spread across ~15-20 full-size sheet pans. Roast at 425°F (220°C) in convection ovens until edges are caramelized and texture is \"al dente.\" \n2. **The Base:** In a tilting brazier or large steam-jacketed kettle, sauté onions and garlic until translucent. Add crushed tomatoes and herbs. Simmer for 45 minutes to develop a thick, rich sauce.\n3. **The Marriage:** Fold the roasted vegetables into the tomato base. Heat through for only 10-15 minutes to allow flavors to meld without breaking down the vegetable structure.\n\nWORKFLOW:\n1. **Prep Station (Hours 1-3):** Set up 3 prep cooks. One on a vegetable slicer/robot-coupe (using the dicing grid) for onions and peppers. Two on manual dicing for eggplant and squash to ensure uniform cubes that won't pulverize.\n2. **Roasting Station (Hours 3-4):** Stagger sheet pans into ovens. Rotate every 10 minutes. \n3. **Sauce Station (Hours 3-4.5):** Start tomato base in the brazier simultaneously with roasting.\n4. **Assembly & Holding (Hour 5):** Combine components. Transfer to 2-inch hotel pans. \n5. **Garnish:** Add fresh basil and a drizzle of raw EVOO just before service.\n\nTIMING:\n*   **Prep/Dicing:** 3 hours (Bulk dicing 80 lbs of produce).\n*   **Roasting:** 1.5 hours (convection rotation).\n*   **Simmering Base:** 1 hour.\n*   **Final Assembly/Meld:** 20 minutes.\n*   **Total Lead Time:** 5-6 hours. \n*   *Note: Allow 45 minutes for large-scale temperature recovery when adding cold/room temp vegetables to the sauce.*\n\nCOST ESTIMATE:\n*   **Produce (Eggplant, Zucchini, Peppers, Onions):** $125.00\n*   **Pantry (Oil, Garlic, Herbs, Vinegar):** $45.00\n*   **Canned Goods (#10 Tomatoes):** $30.00\n*   **Total Estimated Wholesale Cost:** $200.00\n*   **Cost Per Plate (Portion):** ~$3.33\n\nPITFALLS & MITIGATIONS:\n*   **Pitfall: Excess Moisture/Soggy Texture.** *Mitigation:* Salting the eggplant 30 minutes before roasting to draw out water; roasting at high heat instead of boiling in sauce.\n*   **Pitfall: Oversalted Base.** *Mitigation:* Because sauce reduces, season the tomato base lightly; add the final 20% of salt only after the vegetables and sauce are combined.\n*   **Pitfall: Greasy Finish.** *Mitigation:* Eggplant acts like a sponge. Ensure oil is tossed thoroughly on sheet pans rather than poured into the stewing pot.\n*   **Pitfall: Holding Temp.** *Mitigation:* If holding in a hot box (Cambro), keep the lids slightly cracked to prevent steam from further cooking the vegetables into a puree.",
